---
source: crates/dr-agent-tools/tests/catalog.rs
expression: table
---
act.create [write] Create an act starting at a chapter (or at the head of the book).
act.delete [write] Delete an act boundary (its chapters join the act before).
act.list [read] List acts in reading order, each with its chapters.
act.update [write] Rename an act, recolour it, move its boundary or bind an idea to it.
asset.get [read] Read an asset's recorded metadata: kind, media type, size and pixel dimensions (the Stats tab's file readings).
book.get [read] Read a book's name, summary, word targets and counts.
book.search [read] Search a book: titles, summaries, prose, material descriptions and element patches (chapters in reading order first, then ideas, elements, storylines, categories, materials). One- and two-character queries work.
book.update [write] Change a book's name, summary or word targets.
book.word_counts [read] Word counts of a book from the derived index: each chapter, idea and storyline, the book (its chapters), and the word targets.
book.writing_days [read] A book's writing days: words written per day (all devices), today, the last seven days, active days and the streak.
category.create [write] Create an element category (a taken name gets a numbered suffix; the final name is returned).
category.list [read] List element categories, with how many live elements each has.
category.restore [write] Bring a category back from the trash, with its elements.
category.trash [write] Move a category to the trash. Never refused: its elements read as uncategorized until it is restored.
category.update [write] Rename a category (a taken name gets a suffix), recolour it or change its layout.
category_template.body [read] Read a category's element template: the paragraphs (body text or H1/H2/H3) its new elements start with.
category_template.list [read] List the fields every new element of a category starts with, in order.
category_template.put [write] Add a field to a category's template or change one (key, value, position). Existing elements keep their fields.
category_template.remove [write] Remove a field from a category's template. Existing elements keep theirs.
category_template.set_body [write] Replace a category's element template (the body its new elements start with; empty removes it). Existing elements keep their bodies. Available for existing template documents; creation is not exposed by the Agent facade yet.
chapter.arrange [write] Set exact coordinates of several chapters on one axis, and move act boundaries with them, in one step (undo of a spread uses it).
chapter.create [write] Create a chapter (a taken title gets a numbered suffix; the final title is returned), optionally placed on story time too. Given a storyline and no text, it starts from that storyline's chapter template.
chapter.list [read] List chapters in reading order, with their act, storylines, words, creation and last change (trashed ones after, when asked).
chapter.move [write] Move a chapter in reading order (it lands before the next chapter or act boundary).
chapter.place [write] Put a chapter at an exact place on the reading-order or story-time axis and, optionally, on another storyline (as the primary one) or on none, in one step.
chapter.restore [write] Bring a chapter back from the trash (its relations and comments come back with it).
chapter.spread [write] Spread out (打散) the chapters on one axis: same order, evenly spaced, act boundaries moved along, in one step.
chapter.trash [write] Move a chapter to the trash. Never refused: its relations are hidden and comments on it shown as unlocated until it is restored; the result lists them.
chapter.update [write] Change a chapter's title, summary, status, place in reading order, narrative position or storylines.
chapter_template.body [read] Read a storyline's chapter template: the paragraphs (body text or H1/H2/H3) a new chapter whose main storyline it is starts with.
chapter_template.set_body [write] Replace a storyline's chapter template (the body new chapters on it start with; empty removes it). Existing chapters keep their text. Available for existing template documents; creation is not exposed by the Agent facade yet.
comment.anchors [read] The stored anchors of the comments on one entity's prose, for an editor laying them out (comments without an anchor are not listed).
comment.create [write] Add a comment or TODO, on an entity, a paragraph, a passage of its prose, a region of an image or PDF, or floating.
comment.list [read] List comments and TODOs (a target in the trash or gone shows as unlocated).
comment.reanchor [write] Put a comment's anchor back where the editor's undo brought its text back (the author's change, synced; it follows the editor's undo, so it is not an entry of the object-level undo).
comment.update [write] Change a comment or TODO: its text, status (resolve / reopen), priority, kind or linked Agent conversation.
element.create [write] Create an element (it starts with its category's template fields, and its element template as the body when no text is given). A name or alias another element already has is refused (automatic links find people by name); the error names that element.
element.list [read] List elements (with a mark on names a merge left duplicated).
element.restore [write] Bring an element back from the trash (a name taken meanwhile gets a suffix).
element.trash [write] Move an element to the trash. Never refused: its relations are hidden and comments on it unlocated until it is restored.
element.update [write] Change an element: name, summary, category, aliases, fields, portrait, relation-graph pin. A name or alias another element has is refused.
fact.list [read] List the book's project facts (项目设定), in order.
fact.put [write] Add a project fact or change one (key, value, position).
fact.remove [write] Remove a project fact.
history.agent_changes [read] List what an Agent session (or one turn) wrote: one entry per settled write, oldest first.
history.points [read] List a prose document's history points (kept 30 days, at most one every 15 minutes of writing), newest first, with the words at each.
history.preview [read] Read a prose document as it was at a history point: its paragraphs (with H1/H2/H3 levels) and its owner's title and summary then.
history.restore_entities [write] Make named entities of a book equal to an earlier version (one commit; the rest of the book is untouched). The undo of every purge.
history.undo_agent [write] Undo what an Agent session (or one turn) wrote: book fields nobody changed since go back, entities it created go to the trash, entities it deleted come back, and prose untouched since is reverted; the rest is reported as conflicts.
idea.create [write] Create an idea (构想); a taken title gets a numbered suffix and the final title is returned.
idea.list [read] List ideas (构想), optionally of one group.
idea.restore [write] Bring an idea back from the trash (its bindings come back with it).
idea.trash [write] Move an idea to the trash. Never refused: acts and time pins bound to it read as unbound until it is restored.
idea.update [write] Change an idea's title, summary, status, group, board position or narrative position.
idea_group.create [write] Create an idea group (groups nest at most two levels).
idea_group.delete [write] Delete an idea group: its sub-groups and ideas move up to its parent.
idea_group.list [read] List idea groups, parents before children.
idea_group.update [write] Rename, recolour or move an idea group (at most two levels).
material.create [write] Add a material: a text note, or an image or PDF whose asset is registered.
material.list [read] List materials in order, optionally of one folder.
material.move [write] Move a material in the list (only its own position changes).
material.update [write] Change a material's title, description, folder or TODO projection.
material_folder.create [write] Create a material folder.
material_folder.delete [write] Delete a material folder: its sub-folders and materials move up to its parent.
material_folder.list [read] List material folders (素材夹), parents before children.
material_folder.update [write] Rename or move a material folder.
memory.add [write] Add a Project Memory entry (plain text; takes effect at once).
memory.list [read] List the book's Project Memory entries.
memory.remove [write] Remove a Project Memory entry (kept as removed; restorable).
memory.restore [write] Bring back a removed Project Memory entry.
memory.update [write] Change a Project Memory entry's text, kind or status.
mention.list [read] List what prose mentions: entity links and embedded materials, from one entity's prose, to one entity, or in the whole book (live ends only, with paragraph and span counts, the most mentioned first; `limit` keeps the top rows).
patch.create [write] Add a patch to an element: a title (optional) and a body, floating or taken from a passage of a chapter or idea.
patch.list [read] List an element's patches: by their source chapter's reading order, then their own order; floating ones last. Invalidated ones are listed too.
patch.move [write] Move a patch among its element's patches (within the same source chapter the order is the patches' own).
patch.update [write] Change a patch's title or body, or the chapter it belongs to (none detaches it).
plot_grid.cell [write] Write one cell of a plot grid (empty text clears it).
plot_grid.clear [write] 清空: clear every label and every cell of a plot grid; its rows, columns and size stay.
plot_grid.create [write] Store a chapter's or idea's plot grid, three rows by three columns with empty labels, before its first edit; an existing grid is returned as it is.
plot_grid.get [read] Read a chapter's or idea's plot grid: rows, columns and the cells' text (an empty 3 × 3 grid when none is stored yet).
plot_grid.line_add [write] Insert a row or column into a plot grid, first or right after another, with a label (empty shows the placeholder).
plot_grid.line_remove [write] Remove a plot grid's row or column with its cells (the last row or column cannot go).
plot_grid.line_update [write] Rename or move a plot grid's row or column (first, or right after another).
plot_grid.size [write] Set the size of a plot grid's cells (120–440 wide, 56–380 high).
prose.link [write] Link anchored ranges of prose documents to entities (anchors, not positions: the undo of automatic links that were taken off).
prose.read [read] Read paragraphs of a prose document (text, block id, heading level).
prose.replace [write] Replace one exact passage of prose with new text (it must occur exactly once, in one paragraph).
prose.restore [write] Restore a prose document to an earlier version, as a new change (history stays).
prose.shape [read] Shape of a prose document: paragraphs, sentences, the share of dialogue, its checklist and the materials it embeds.
prose.unlink [write] Take links to entities off anchored ranges of prose documents (anchors, not positions: the undo of automatic links).
recent.list [read] List the pages of a book the author opened recently on this device, newest first (what is in the trash or gone is left out).
relation.create [write] Relate two entities (a symmetric type stores its ends in canonical order; an existing identical relation is returned instead).
relation.delete [write] Delete a relation.
relation.list [read] List the relations to show (both ends live, one per semantic edge).
relation_type.create [write] Create a relation type. A name another type has is refused (`RT-NAME`); the error names that type.
relation_type.list [read] List relation types (the built-in one is marked locked).
relation_type.update [write] Rename a relation type or change its description, role names or colour (the built-in type is locked).
storyline.create [write] Create a storyline (a taken name gets a numbered suffix; the final name is returned). It starts with the book's storyline template fields.
storyline.list [read] List storylines in order, with how many live chapters each has.
storyline.move [write] Move a storyline in the list (only its own position changes).
storyline.restore [write] Bring a storyline back from the trash, with its chapters' membership.
storyline.trash [write] Move a storyline to the trash. Never refused: chapters keep their membership, hidden until it is restored.
storyline.update [write] Rename a storyline (a taken name gets a suffix), recolour it, change its summary or its fields.
storyline_template.list [read] List the fields every new storyline starts with, in order.
storyline_template.put [write] Add a field to the storyline template or change one (key, value, position). Existing storylines keep their fields.
storyline_template.remove [write] Remove a field from the storyline template. Existing storylines keep theirs.
time_pin.create [write] Put a time pin on the narrative axis, with a label or bound to an idea.
time_pin.delete [write] Delete a time pin.
time_pin.list [read] List the book's time pins along the narrative axis, with the ideas bound to them.
time_pin.update [write] Move a time pin, change its label, or bind it to an idea or unbind it.

